<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    macOS hardened-runtime entitlements for Vibechek.

    Notarized apps MUST run under hardened runtime (Apple started rejecting
    non-hardened builds in early 2020). Hardened runtime locks down a bunch
    of behaviors that the Vibechek sidecar needs — specifically, PyInstaller's
    `--onefile` bootloader extracts the bundled Python framework to a tmpdir
    at startup, and the default library-validation rule blocks the main
    binary from dlopen()-ing those extracted .dylibs.

    The four entitlements below are the standard PyInstaller-on-macOS set:

      - `disable-library-validation`: required so the sidecar can load the
        extracted Python framework from its private tmpdir. Without it,
        every analyze through the sidecar dies at the first essentia /
        mutagen import.
      - `allow-jit` + `allow-unsigned-executable-memory`: keep TensorFlow's
        XLA compiler happy. Not used by the sidecar today (it stays in pure
        Python) but cheap insurance for future ML hooks.
      - `allow-dyld-environment-variables`: required by PyInstaller's
        bootloader to set DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH for the extracted framework.

    `tauri-action`'s macOS signing step picks this up via the entitlements
    field in tauri.conf.json -> bundle.macOS.entitlements. The same file is
    applied to both the main Tauri binary and the sidecar (codesign reuses
    the entitlements for every Mach-O it touches via --options runtime).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
